# Scope -> registry mapping and auth for the private @izzywdev/* packages.
#
# THIS FILE, NOT THE REPO-ROOT ONE, IS THE ONE npm READS HERE. npm resolves a
# project .npmrc at the PROJECT ROOT — the nearest directory up from cwd holding a
# package.json — and services/ui-react has one. FuzeAgent has no root package.json,
# so the repo-root .npmrc is never consulted for this install. That is why adding
# the mapping at the root alone did not help.
#
# @izzywdev is the scope npm actually FETCHES. GitHub Packages requires the scope to
# match the owning account, so publishing renames @fuzefront/* -> @izzywdev/fuzefront-*
# and a plain @fuzefront/... semver 404s.
@izzywdev:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
@fuzefront:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com

# Authenticate from the environment; never commit a token. In CI this is the
# workflow's own GITHUB_TOKEN, which can read packages owned by the same account
# (verified: FuzeX's design-frames-service-ci installs private @izzywdev/* packages
# with exactly this token). The _authToken line is the load-bearing one -- the 401
# this fixes was on the tarball DOWNLOAD url, with "authentication token not
# provided". always-auth mirrors the fleet convention (see FuzeBI's .npmrc); npm 10
# still accepts it, though the token line alone may be sufficient on modern npm.
#
# An UNSET GITHUB_TOKEN does not break npm -- it resolves to empty and you get the
# same 401 as today, not a config error. Verified with npm 10.9.7.
//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=${GITHUB_TOKEN}
always-auth=true
